00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:36.020
I think a lot of our cold opens were things also that might be feeling a little too absurd
对。

292
00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:42.600
for our little documentary show that we were putting on, but we could be a little more broad
对。

293
00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:48.800
in our cold opens as well, which brings us to, speaking of a little bit of a broader cold open,
对。

294
00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:50.240
Angela, your pick.
对。

295
00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:51.920
It's one of our classics.
对。

296
00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:57.340
So I wanted to pick a cold open that was a real all ensemble moment, because sometimes the
对。

297
00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:01.260
cold opens were mostly, you know, Michael or Dwight and those kind of storylines.
对。

298
00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:07.100
But this had everybody, it included a huge rehearsal with our camera operators, our boom
对。

299
00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:07.540
operators.
对。

300
00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.880
It was like a real dance, if you will.
对。

301
00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.440
It's something I get yelled at the airport, okay?
这是我在机场经常被吼的话，好吧？

302
00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:18.420
So basically, this is from Stress Relief, the episode Stress Relief.
所以基本上，这出自《压力释放》，那一集叫《压力释放》。

303
00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:24.140
It is that famous fire drill cold open where Dwight is going to teach everyone how to survive
就是那个著名的火灾演习开场白，德怀特要在里面教大家如何在大火中生存

304
00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:25.560
an office place fire.
办公室的火灾。

305
00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.360
By setting a fire.
通过放火。

306
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:30.500
Yes, by locking the doors and setting a fire.
对，通过锁上门然后放火。

307
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:32.680
It's absolute instant mayhem.
简直是一场瞬间的混乱。

308
00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:38.100
We pulled a clip from the middle where Dwight is trying to get everyone to do all the steps
我们截取了中间的一段剪辑，当时德怀特正试图让大家在混乱中

309
00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:38.800
in the chaos.
完成所有的步骤。

310
00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:39.620
Here it is.
就是这个。

311
00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.460
Have you ever seen a burn victim?
你们见过烧伤患者吗？

312
00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:42.520
Move it!
快动起来！

313
00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:44.740
Okay, procedure, procedure.
好的，按程序来，按程序来。

314
00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:46.700
Excellent options.
很棒的选择。

315
00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.100
Where do we go, folks?
我们去哪儿，各位？

316
00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:49.860
Use a what to cover the mouth?
用什么捂住嘴？

317
00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:50.280
A what?
用什么？

318
00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:52.960
A rag, a damp rag, perhaps.
抹布，一块湿抹布，也许吧。

319
00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:55.460
Let's remember those procedures.
让我们记住这些程序。

320
00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:56.480
What are the options?
有哪些选择？

321
00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:58.340
That's the wrong way.
那条路不对。

322
00:14:58.440 --> 00:14:59.440
We've already tried that.
我们已经试过了。

323
00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.400
Remember your exit points.
记住你们的逃生出口。

324
00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:02.680
Exit points, people.
逃生出口，各位。

325
00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:03.240
What's next?
接下来是什么？

326
00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:03.600
Oscar!
奥斯卡！

327
00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:03.920
Huh?
啊？

328
00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:04.780
Stay alive.
活下去。

329
00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:05.440
I'm getting help.
我去求救。

330
00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:06.260
Pull me up.
拉我上去。

331
00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:07.040
You're too heavy.
你太重了。

332
00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:15.960
Come on, anyone?
拜托，有人吗？

333
00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:16.740
9-1-1.
911。

334
00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:21.360
Oh my God.
我的天哪。

335
00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.440
I mean, it's so ridiculous.
我的意思是，这太荒谬了。

336
00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:22.980
It's so good.
太棒了。

337
00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:24.940
It's so great.
太伟大了。

338
00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:26.380
Can I ask a question?
我能问个问题吗？

339
00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.720
How long did that take to shoot that?
拍那段花了多长时间？

340
00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:29.180
That looks like an entire movie.
看起来就像拍了一整部电影。

341
00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:33.740
Well, it was a full day to shoot it, but it was also a bunch of rehearsal.
嗯，拍了一整天，但也排练了很多次。

342
00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:34.280
Yeah.
是啊。

343
00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:41.760
I came in a day early, and I had to work with this cat and the cat wrangler, and we had a fake cat.
我提前一天来了，我得和这只猫还有驯猫师一起工作，我们有一只假猫。

344
00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:46.720
We had a real cat and a fake cat, and I had a body double.
我们有一只真猫和一只假猫，我还有一个替身。

345
00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:52.940
Like, that stunt, just that moment of throwing the cat in the ceiling and it coming out the other side was a huge stunt.
就像那个特技，仅仅是把猫扔进天花板又从另一边掉出来的那个瞬间，就是一个巨大的特技。

346
00:15:53.180 --> 00:16:05.240
I just love, like, the unexpected, like, the first throw of the cat is hilarious and unexpected, but so soon after is that second falling cat, which you're not quite expecting,
我特别喜欢那种出乎意料的感觉，比如第一次扔猫既搞笑又出人意料，但紧接着又是第二次猫掉下来的场景，这是你完全意料不到的，

347
00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:08.940
which is like the actual enormous laugh of that moment.
这可以说是那一刻真正的巨大笑点。

348
00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:10.480
And the cat was totally okay.
而且那只猫完全没事。

349
00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:17.160
Everyone listening, our line producer, had put a whole bunch of, like, Fernie pad things, so he did hit something squishy and soft.
听着的各位，我们的制片主任放了很多像费尼垫一样的东西，所以他确实撞到了一个又软又捏起来有弹性的东西。

350
00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:25.500
I love also, though, Rain's unflappable kind of commitment to, like, saying everyone is absolutely panicking around him.
不过我也很喜欢雷恩那种镇定自若的投入，就好像周围所有人都陷入了绝对的恐慌一样。

351
00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:34.880
I heard Adam McKay say this thing, that there's nothing funnier than people being confidently wrong, and so much of Dwight is confident wrongness,
我听亚当·麦凯说过这么一句话，他说世界上没有什么比一个人自信满满却又错得离谱更搞笑的事了，德怀特身上就有很多这种自信的错误，

352
00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:40.100
and so much of, like, the humor on The Office is confident, absolute wrongness.
《办公室》里也有很多这种充满自信的、绝对的错误幽默。

353
00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:42.260
Well, that's definitely Michael Scott.
嗯，那绝对就是迈克尔·斯科特。

354
00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.180
He's, like, the most confident idiot ever.
他就像是有史以来最自信的笨蛋。

355
00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:47.560
There's something about us.
我们身上确实有某种特质。

356
00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:48.160
It's true.
确实如此。

357
00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.580
We love that confident wrongness.
我们就是喜欢那种自信的错误。

358
00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:50.900
Yeah.
对。

359
00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:59.320
All right, so we asked you guys to pull your favorite cold opens, and I know there's, it's always so hard to pick one, but Josh, which one did you pick?
好了，我们让大家挑出自己最喜欢的片头前情提要，我知道挑选一个总是很难，但是乔许，你选了哪个？

360
00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.100
So I picked an episode from season four.
我选的是第四季的一集。

361
00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:04.280
It's called Right Place, Right Time.
剧名叫《命中注定》。

362
00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:07.080
I think it was episode 22 from season four.
我想那是第四季的第 22 集。

363
00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:15.360
It kind of starts off in a little bit of a minor key in terms of, you know, Robin and Ted and this morning scene, and he's running out to, he's going to go get a bagel,
一开场带点小调子的感觉，你知道的，罗宾和泰德还有这个早晨的场景，他正跑出去，打算去买个百吉饼，

364
00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:18.420
like a very kind of low-key New York morning.
就像是一个非常低调的纽约早晨。

365
00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:24.440
And then he goes out the front door, and he's about to turn one way, but he stops, and he turns another way.
然后他走出了前门，正准备往一个方向拐，但他停了下来，又转向了另一个方向。

366
00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:25.760
He goes another direction.
他朝另一个方向走去。

367
00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:32.200
And then the narrator comes in and starts talking about, you know, almost the butterfly effect, essentially, like,
接着旁白切进来，开始谈论，你知道的，基本上可以说是蝴蝶效应，

368
00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:40.440
our seemingly innocuous choices are all kind of adding up together in this magical way to deliver us where we need to be.
我们那些看似微不足道的选择，都在以这种神奇的方式汇聚起来，把我们带到我们需要去的地方。

369
00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:47.420
And also, right when he leaves, he grabs the yellow umbrella, which at that point we had established as an iconic prop.
而且，他一出门就拿了那把黄雨伞，当时我们已经把这设定为一个标志性的道具。

370
00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:51.420
Yeah, but he told the audience that is involved in how Ted is going to meet the mother eventually.
对，但他告诉观众，这与泰德最终如何遇到母亲有关。

371
00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:53.260
That's like this magical, like, little flash forward.
这就好比是一个神奇的小闪回。

372
00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.880
We saw a yellow umbrella as part of that meeting.
我们看到黄雨伞是那次相遇的一部分。

373
00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:56.260
Right.
没错。

374
00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:02.860
So he's like, he's just going outside to get a bagel, but he grabs this yellow umbrella, and then it's, oh my God, like, now this is very, very significant.
所以他就像是，他只是出门买个百吉饼，但他拿了这把黄雨伞，然后就是，天哪，现在这变得非常非常重要了。

375
00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:10.460
And then it starts to rain, and he opens the umbrella, and then he gets a tap on his shoulder, and he turns, and we don't know who he's seeing.
然后开始下雨，他撑开雨伞，接着有人拍了一下他的肩膀，他转过身，我们不知道他看到了谁。

376
00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.440
But the fake out, of course, is this must be the mother.
但这个障眼法，当然了，让人觉得这肯定就是母亲。

377
00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:14.260
Right.
对。

378
00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:16.960
And that's when you cut to main titles, and you don't see the face.
然后画面就切到片头，你看不到她的脸。

379
00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.380
You have to watch the whole episode to see who's behind that umbrella.
你必须看完整集才能知道那把雨伞后面的人是谁。

380
00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:25.340
I loved this cold open, and I also love the voiceover, which is by Bob Saget.
我很喜欢这个片头前情提要，我也很喜欢鲍勃·萨吉特配音的旁白。

381
00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:25.700
Yeah.
对。

382
00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.660
So I would love to hear that, because it's poetic and wonderful, and I love it.
所以我很想听听那段，因为充满诗意又无比美妙，我超爱。

383
00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:37.190
Kids, I've been telling you the story of how I met your mother.
孩子们，我一直在给你们讲我是怎么遇到你们母亲的故事。

384
00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:41.230
And while there's many things to learn from this story, this may be the biggeSt.
虽然这个故事里有很多东西可以学，但这个可能最重要。

385
00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:45.170
The great moments of your life won't necessarily be the things you do.
你人生中的高光时刻不一定是你做过的事。

386
00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:47.690
They'll also be the things that happen to you.
它们也同样是你经历过的事。

387
00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:51.850
Now, I'm not saying you can't take action to affect the outcome of your life.
我并不是说你不能采取行动去影响你人生的结果。

388
00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:54.610
You have to take action, and you will.
你必须采取行动，而且你也会这么做。

389
00:18:55.710 --> 00:19:01.390
But never forget that on any day, you can step out the front door, and your whole life can change forever.
但永远不要忘记，在任何一天，你走出前门，你的整个人生都可能永远改变。

390
00:19:02.330 --> 00:19:04.250
You see, the universe has a plan, kids.
你看，命运自有安排，孩子们。

391
00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:06.450
And that plan is always in motion.
而且这个计划一直在进行中。

392
00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.950
A butterfly flaps its wings, and it starts to rain.
蝴蝶扇动翅膀，然后就开始下雨。

393
00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:15.550
It's a scary thought, but it's also kind of wonderful.
这是一个可怕的想法，但同时也挺美妙的。

394
00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:22.090
All these little parts of the machine constantly working, making sure that you end up exactly where you're supposed to be,
所有这些机器的小零件都在不断运转，确保你最终到达你该去的地方，

395
00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:23.890
exactly when you're supposed to be there.
就在你该到达的那个确切时间。

396
00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:27.570
The right place, at the right time.
在对的时间，去对的地方。

397
00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:29.910
Aw, that is so beautiful.
啊，那真是太美了。

398
00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:36.930
Yeah, Craig, I think that's some of the most, like, kind of lyrical, narrating, writing that you and the gang did.
对，克雷格，我觉得那是你和伙伴们写过的最具有抒情色彩的旁白台词之一。

399
00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:45.050
And I also, it really strikes me that the entire DNA, like, blueprint of the entire series is in that cold open.
而且我也觉得，整个剧集的全部基因、也就是蓝图，都包含在那个片头里了。

400
00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:45.990
Yeah.
对。

401
00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:46.270
Right?
对吧？

402
00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:46.730
Yeah.
对。

403
00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:51.530
The whole show is spelled out thematically and illustrated in that.
整部剧的主题都在其中阐明并通过它展现出来。

404
00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:56.210
And then as the episode goes on, again, we talk a lot about How I Met Your Mother.
然后随着剧集的发展，我们再次大量谈到《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

405
00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:57.890
One, it's a big mystery.
第一，这是一个巨大的谜团。

406
00:19:58.050 --> 00:19:59.410
It's a nine-season mystery.
这是一个历时九季的谜团。

407
00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:05.210
But also, a lot of episodes start off with something very strange and disorienting and what is going on.
但同时，很多集都是从一些非常奇怪、让人摸不着头脑、不知所云的场景开始的。

408
00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.390
And then the episode solves the mystery.
然后这一集会解开这个谜团。

409
00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.630
So it's like mysteries inside mysteries inside mysteries.
所以这就像是谜中之谜、套中套。

410
00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:10.130
Yeah.
对。

411
00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:17.050
And this is like, this is a great mystery episode, but it's also, it just lays out all the themes so beautifully at the beginning.
而且这就像，这是一集超棒的悬疑剧，但同时它又在一开始就把所有的主题都展现得如此美妙。

412
00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:19.270
And Bob, man, I love hearing Bob's voice.
鲍勃，天哪，我太喜欢听鲍勃的声音了。

413
00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:23.390
I get such a chill note hearing Bob do that one.
听鲍勃读那段独白，我真的起了一身鸡皮疙瘩。

414
00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:25.790
He was such a secret weapon of the show.
他是这部剧里的一张秘密王牌。

415
00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:29.530
He was so good at being future Ted, telling his kids that story.
他太擅长演年长的泰德，向孩子们讲那个故事了。

416
00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:29.910
Yeah.
是啊。

417
00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:35.850
And I think it's an underrated kind of, like, track in the overall song of our show.
而且我认为这是我们整部剧的主旋律中，一种被低估的音轨。

418
00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:39.410
You know, that's like a really crucial instrument in the orchestra there.
你知道的，那就像是交响乐团中非常关键的一件乐器。

419
00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:40.750
And he was wonderful.
而且他太棒了。

420
00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:41.810
It makes me miss him.
这让我很想念他。

421
00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:42.770
He was just a great guy.
他真的是个很好的人。

422
00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:44.250
And he loved playing that part.
而且他很喜欢演这个角色。

423
00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:46.370
He took a lot of pride in being future Ted.
他为能扮演年长的泰德感到非常自豪。

424
00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:49.790
He and Josh developed a real bond because he's like, I'm future you.
他和乔希建立了一种真正的羁绊，因为他说，我是未来的你。

425
00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:50.890
I'm the voice of future you.
我是未来你的声音。

426
00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:53.210
And you guys, like, really became close over that.
你们两个因为这个，关系真的变得很亲密。

427
00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:53.950
And you hear it.
你听得出来。

428
00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:55.170
He would watch the episode.
他会看这一集。

429
00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.310
He'd tap into where Ted was emotionally.
他能体会到泰德当时的情感状态。

430
00:20:57.590 --> 00:20:59.150
And he'd perform that.
然后他会把那种感觉表演出来。

431
00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:00.570
And I love that about him.
我太喜欢他这一点了。

432
00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.450
And yeah, this is kind of the whole series in miniature.
而且，是的，这有点像是整个系列的微缩版。

433
00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:11.210
Since this whole episode that you're having us on is a symposium on cold opens, this is another way we used cold opens, which is we liked teasing a mystery.
既然你邀请我们来参加的这一整集是对开场白（cold open）的研讨，这也是我们使用开场白的另一种方式，那就是我们喜欢制造一个悬念。

434
00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:15.230
We liked really getting right up to a mystery moment, hard cutting the credits.
我们喜欢把悬念推到最高潮，然后立刻切入片头字幕。

435
00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:20.050
And you have to stay tuned for the next 20 minutes to find out, in this case, who's behind that umbrella.
而你必须在接下来的20分钟里继续收看，才能发现在这个桥段里，那把伞后面到底是谁。

436
00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:22.010
It turns out to be Meryl Streep playing herself.
结果发现是梅丽尔·斯特里普在演她自己。

437
00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:22.770
I know.
我知道。

438
00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:25.690
But you need to watch the rest of the episode.
但你需要把剩下的一集看完。

439
00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:33.370
And that's what I mean by we usually put the cold open on How I Met Your Mother was like a load-bearing support of the architecture.
这就是我的意思，我们在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中通常会把开场白当作整个结构的承重支柱。

440
00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.670
Because we really start the story there.
因为我们确实是从那里开始讲故事的。

441
00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:38.210
We plant a mystery or we kick something off in some way.
我们埋下一个谜团，或者以某种方式拉开序幕。

442
00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:41.630
We didn't do as many freestanders as you guys did, which, again, are really hard to do.
我们不像你们那样做那么多独立的开场，说实话，那种真的很难做。

443
00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.470
One other thing I want to say about that cold open.
关于那个开场白，我还想说一件事。

444
00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:51.830
Again, you know, when Craig and I have been doing How I Met Your Mother, part of it is like the mystery of why people love the show so much.
再说一次，你知道的，当我和克雷格一直在制作《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，其中一部分就像是一个谜，为什么人们会这么喜欢这部剧。

445
00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:56.110
And why do people still want to talk about them and play them for their kids and all this stuff.
为什么人们到现在还想谈论它，放给自己的孩子看等等。

446
00:21:56.490 --> 00:22:03.550
And one of the things that I think people find so much solace in is that it's a meaningful universe.
我认为人们在其中找到巨大慰藉的原因之一，就是它是一个充满意义的宇宙。

447
00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:10.470
How I Met Your Mother says the universe, both in this show and presumably the one that you're existing in also, is meaningful.
《老爸老妈浪漫史》告诉我们，无论是在这部剧里，还是在你所在的现实世界中，宇宙都是有意义的。

448
00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.830
And every little encounter has meaning.
每一个小小的相遇都有它的意义。

449
00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:15.710
And it's kind of an antidote to cynicism.
这简直是愤世嫉俗的解药。

450
00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:19.130
It's an antidote to nihilism, you know, or chaos.
你知道的，这是虚无主义或混乱的解药。

451
00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:22.950
That there's a plan and there's benevolence and there's a guiding hand.
那就是这里有计划、有仁慈、有一只指引的手。

452
00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:29.930
And I think that even if people don't articulate it that way, that's part of the warmth they feel when they watch the show and why they keep coming back to it.
我想，即使人们没有那样表达出来，这也是他们看这部剧时感受到的温暖的一部分，也是他们不断回看的原因。

453
00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:31.150
I so agree.
我太赞同了。

454
00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:32.790
It's like comfort, you know.
就像是一种安慰，你知道的。

455
00:22:32.790 --> 00:22:37.910
When I watch your show, it makes me feel like everything's okay in some way.
当我看你们的剧时，某种程度上让我觉得一切都好。

456
00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:43.230
And I want you guys to know, just yesterday I met a woman who told me she loves your podcaSt.
我想让你们知道，就在昨天我遇到了一位女士，她告诉我她非常喜欢你们的播客。

457
00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:44.670
She's an Office Ladies listener.
她是《办公室女士》的听众。

458
00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:45.690
She's so excited.
她超级激动。

459
00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:46.710
It's on our network.
它在我们的网络上。

460
00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:56.470
And then she told me that her and her husband recreated the moment with the umbrella, with the trench coat, where you only see the legs, you know.
然后她告诉我，她和她丈夫重现了那个撑着伞、穿着风衣、只能看到腿的场景，你知道的。

461
00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:58.930
And she pulled out her phone and showed it to me.
接着她掏出手机给我看了看。

462
00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:03.410
And then I was like, I'm actually seeing them tomorrow and we're watching this cold open.
然后我说，我明天就要见到他们了，我们正要看这段片头前情提要。

463
00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:07.670
So I just think that your show is really meaningful to people.
所以我只是觉得你们的剧对人们真的很有意义。

464
00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:08.930
And that's a special thing.
而这是一件很特别的事。

465
00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:10.470
We need those touchstones.
我们需要这些精神寄托。

466
00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:12.070
I think we're two for two.
我觉得我们两个都说中了。

467
00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:13.330
We're hitting them out of the park.
我们表现得太棒了。

468
00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:15.010
And so, okay, who's up next?
那么，好吧，接下来轮到谁了？

469
00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:15.950
Jenna, it's you.
珍娜，轮到你了。

470
00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:16.790
Sure.
好的。

471
00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:21.910
Well, I felt a little shy about picking this one because I am the star of it.
嗯，我选这个觉得有点不好意思，因为我是主角。

472
00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:27.210
But I only ever got to star in one cold open and it is one of my favorites.
但我只主演过一个片头前情提要，而且它是我最喜欢的之一。

473
00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:28.990
It was in season nine.
它在第九季。

474
00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:34.490
And it also features the whole ensemble because those were also my favorite cold opens.
而且它还全员出镜，因为这也是我最喜欢的片头前情提要类型。

475
00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:38.890
You know, a lot of our cold opens were a great opportunity for Jim to play a prank on Dwight.
你知道的，我们的很多片头前情提要都是吉姆捉弄德怀特的好机会。

476
00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:40.970
Those are some of our most famous ones.
那些是我们最出名的一些。

477
00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:45.070
You know, Bears Beats, Battlestar Galactica, when he puts his desk in the bathroom.
你知道的，熊、甜菜、太空堡垒卡拉狄加，还有他把办公桌搬进浴室的时候。

478
00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:50.590
Or it features Michael doing something crazy like parkour or needing to put his face in cement.
或者是以迈克尔做一些疯狂的事情为特色，比如跑酷或者需要把脸埋进水泥里。

479
00:23:50.590 --> 00:23:53.610
And so, this one was really fun for me.
所以，这个对我来说真的很有趣。

480
00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:55.330
It's Pam's chore wheel.
这是帕姆的家务轮盘。

481
00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:57.910
And it's near and dear to my heart.
这对我来说意义非凡。

482
00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:03.310
If there was one prop that I wish I had from the show, it would have been any of these chore wheels.
如果说有什么道具我希望从剧里拥有的话，那就是这些家务轮盘中的任何一个。

483
00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:05.130
I wish I had that hanging in my office.
我希望我的办公室里也挂着这个。

484
00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:09.750
But basically, what happens in this is the office is really dirty.
但基本上，这里发生的事情是办公室真的很脏。

485
00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:10.870
It's messy.
很乱。

486
00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:14.110
I mean, you even see like a little rat crawl across the floor.
我是说，你甚至能看到一只小老鼠在地板上爬过。

487
00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:21.490
And Pam's the office administrator, and she has to try to come up with some sort of cleaning schedule where people divide up all of the chores.
而帕姆是办公室行政人员，她不得不尝试制定某种清洁时间表，让大家分担所有的家务。

488
00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:23.890
So, she tries making a chore wheel.
所以，她试着做一个家务轮盘。

489
00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:26.750
We have a little clip from the top of this one.
我们有一小段来自这个片段开头的剪辑。

490
00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:27.690
We can take a listen.
我们可以听一下。

491
00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:34.230
The building's custodian is on vacation for the month, and Dwight is too cheap to hire a replacement.
大楼的门卫这个月在休假，而德怀特又太抠门不肯请人代班。

492
00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:36.490
So, instead, we're living in filth.
所以，我们只能生活在污秽之中。

493
00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:37.890
But not for long.
不过不会太久了。

494
00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:41.370
Because I have created the chore wheel.
因为我制作了家务轮盘。

495
00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:42.850
Oh, yeah.
哦，耶。

496
00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:44.370
Can I spin first?
我能先转吗？

497
00:24:44.670 --> 00:24:45.770
Well, it doesn't spin.
呃，它不转的。

498
00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:50.990
We'll just move the wheel one notch each morning, and you see what chore you get that day.
我们每天早晨只把轮盘拨动一格，然后你就会看到那天你分到什么家务。

499
00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:52.630
A wheel is supposed to spin.
轮盘本来就该是用来转的。

500
00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:53.070
Yeah.
是啊。

501
00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:56.250
You know, like, gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu-gu.
你知道的，就像是，咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕-咕。

502
00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:57.370
No, I'm familiar with spinning.
不，我知道旋转是怎么回事。

503
00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:00.970
It's just that wouldn't work with a chore wheel, because people might get the same chore.
只是这用在轮值表上行不通，因为大家可能会抽到同一样杂务。

504
00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:02.330
Bah, boring.
切，真无聊。

505
00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:04.210
All she talks about is chores.
她满嘴都是杂务。

506
00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:05.730
A wheel wants to spin, Pam.
转盘就是用来转的，帕姆。

507
00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.230
Spinning would be more fun.
旋转会更有趣。

508
00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:13.990
Okay.
好吧。

509
00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:17.170
Okay, that's what I'm talking about.
好吧，这正合我意。

510
00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:20.450
Big money, big money.
大奖，大奖。

511
00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:30.970
So, she makes a spinning wheel, and then they get mad because the only thing that you can win is a chore.
所以，她做一个转盘，然后大家就很生气，因为上面唯一能抽到的只有杂务。

512
00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:41.710
So, then she has to make another wheel where you win fun stuff, but there's one thing on the wheel that's called tiny wheel, and if you hit tiny wheel,
于是，她不得不做另一个转盘，上面能赢得好玩的东西，但转盘上有一项叫作迷你转盘，如果你转中了迷你转盘，

513
00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:45.590
you have to spin a tinier chore wheel that's really cute.
你就得转一个超级可爱的小号杂务转盘。

514
00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:52.270
And that has actual chores on it, but Pam says no one cares because the tiny wheel is so cute.
上面写着真正的杂务，但帕姆说没人介意，因为这个迷你转盘太可爱了。

515
00:25:55.210 --> 00:26:00.650
And it was just funny and, you know, silly, and I don't know, I love it.
这真的很好笑，而且你知道，挺傻气的，总之，我太喜欢了。

516
00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:02.230
I would love a chore wheel in real life.
现实生活中我也好想要一个杂务转盘。

517
00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:07.950
I was going to say, Jenna, I don't know you that well yet, but this seems to me like entirely door-to-door something you would do.
我想说的是，珍娜，虽然我对你还不够了解，但这绝对是你能干出来的事。

518
00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:09.250
Am I right about that?
我没说错吧？

519
00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:09.930
Yes, bingo.
没错，完全说中。

520
00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:15.610
I think it's another reason why she loves it is because in real life, Jenna, you do make a chore liSt.
我觉得她喜欢这个的另一个原因是因为在现实生活中，珍娜，你确实会列一张杂务清单。

521
00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:16.510
I love Virgo, Jenna.
我爱处女座，珍娜。

522
00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:17.810
I'm a Pisces.
我是双鱼座。

523
00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:20.970
I'm a very organized Pisces.
我是个非常有条理的双鱼座。

524
00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:22.850
The tiny wheel feels very you.
那个迷你转盘太符合你的风格了。

525
00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:24.810
I feel like you would definitely do the tiny wheel part.
我觉得你绝对会搞出迷你转盘这一出。

526
00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:25.770
We're going to make this fun.
我们要把它变得有趣。

527
00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:26.770
We got a tiny wheel.
我们有个迷你转盘。

528
00:26:26.930 --> 00:26:28.030
Yeah, it's super tiny.
对，超小的。

529
00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:34.010
And I think it just also, it's like having kids and trying to get them excited about chores.
而且我觉得这也像是有了孩子之后，试图让他们对做家务感到兴奋。

530
00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:41.850
Like there's all these adults in the room, but they're just acting like children, and she's having to like manipulate them into keeping their own space clean.
就像屋里的这些成年人，但他们表现得像个孩子，而她不得不像操纵小孩一样哄他们保持自己区域的清洁。

531
00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:44.370
It's like, don't you want it to be clean, guys?
就像在说，大家难道不希望这里干净吗？

532
00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:48.870
It's also fun to play as an actor when it's like you against everyone else.
作为演员，当这种戏份演到你一个人对抗所有人时，也挺好玩的。

533
00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:55.730
How everyone unites as like a single hive mind where they're like, no, it should spin, like a circle should spin, a wheel should spin.
每个人都团结成一个蜂群意识，都在说，不行，它得转，圆圈就该转，转盘就该转。

534
00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:57.550
Even Jim doesn't have her back.
连吉姆都不支持她。

535
00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:59.370
Even Jim turns on you.
连吉姆都反戈一击。

536
00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:08.510
But it's a little like, I always, like Simpsons crowd scenes are so funny when everyone like picks up like pitchforks and lanterns and just like storms City Hall or whatever.
但这有点像，我一直觉得，辛辣里的群众场面太搞笑了，每个人都拿起草叉和提灯，就像这样冲向市政厅什么的。

537
00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:10.810
So quickly over anything.
为了一点小事就这么快。

538
00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:17.130
And it kind of shows like our ability to fall into like mass psychosis as a society, you know?
这也某种程度上展示了我们社会陷入集体歇斯底里的能力，你懂的？

539
00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:23.090
I loved as part of the ensemble whenever we would all collectively like react to one character.
我特别喜欢作为群演的那种感觉，就是我们所有人一起对某一个角色作出集体反应。

540
00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:24.330
I loved it so much.
我真的太喜欢了。

541
00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:27.870
I mean, Pam rarely had to hold everyone's attention in the conference room.
我的意思是，帕姆很少需要在会议室里吸引所有人的注意力。

542
00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:29.110
That was normally Michael, right?
那通常是迈克尔的活儿，对吧？

543
00:27:29.290 --> 00:27:29.890
All the time.
每次都是。

544
00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:34.470
But it was so fun to just be like, be in the crowd and be like, we don't get it.
但能作为人群的一员，附和着说“我们不懂啊”真的很好玩。

545
00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:35.130
I don't get it.
我不懂。

546
00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:35.630
Do you get it?
你懂吗？

547
00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:36.290
I don't get it.
我不懂。

548
00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:37.570
It was super fun.
真的超级好玩。

549
00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:41.350
I remember it was very intimidating to stand in front of you all too.
我记得站在你们所有人面前其实挺让人紧张的。

550
00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:46.310
I was standing there and I was like, oh my gosh, Steve did this for seven years.
我站在那儿心想，天哪，史蒂夫竟然这样干了七年。

551
00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:48.330
How did he do this?
他是怎么做到的？

552
00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:51.550
But don't you feel like that's how Pam would feel?
但你不觉得潘会是这种感觉吗？

553
00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:53.050
Like that feels very usable.
就感觉这个挺实用的。

554
00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:56.410
Like where actor thing and character thing are probably close.
就像演员本身和角色可能挺接近的。

555
00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:57.110
Yes.
对。

556
00:27:57.330 --> 00:28:00.890
Like she has like this really great idea that she feels really confident about.
就像她有一个真的很棒的想法，而且她对此信心十足。

557
00:28:01.050 --> 00:28:06.750
But also as soon as anybody pushes back against her, she's like, oh no.
但同时只要有人反驳她，她就会想，哦不。

558
00:28:07.570 --> 00:28:09.190
Craig, I think it's your turn.
克雷格，我觉得轮到你了。

559
00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:11.470
You're the person who hasn't shared yet.
你是那个还没分享的人。

560
00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:12.690
I'm very nervous.
我很紧张。

561
00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:12.770
Yes.
是的。

562
00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:17.850
Now, the funny thing is we've, this is by far the simplest one of any of the ones that
有趣的是，我们，这是我们今天讨论过的所有方案中

563
00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:18.510
we talked about today.
最简单的一个。

564
00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:24.070
I chose it mainly because I'm looking at it through the lens of doing our podcast, of doing
我选它主要是因为我是从做播客的角度来看的，也就是做

